{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790987143,"nonce":"ZXkRfie8HmGiLKGgFCS2HThNuns=","message":"Fixed in 2222fc45.\n\nYour diagnosis was right and the problem was wider. All four non-linear\nvariants evaluated a different curve from the one the same theme exports as\nCSS, not just CubicBezier:\n\n- EaseIn computed t*t while state.rs exported cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)\n- EaseOut computed 1-(1-t)^2 while exporting cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1)\n- EaseInOut was a quad in/out while exporting cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)\n- CubicBezier ignored its x pair, as you found\n\nSo the fix is one source of truth rather than a better solver: Easing::\ncontrol_points() returns the CSS control points, evaluate() solves through\nthem, and the CSS exporter reads the same method. The two cannot drift again.\n\nNo new solver was written. blinc_animation already had a correct one, Newton\nwith a bisection fallback, and blinc_theme already depends on that crate, so\nevaluate defers to blinc_animation::Easing::CubicBezier(..).apply(t).\n\nFive tests, three of which fail on the old code reporting exactly the 0.5375\nin this issue: STANDARD against CSS's 0.8024; two curves differing only in x\nmust not agree; every named variant must match its own exported control\npoints; a spring curve must still exceed 1, since only the input is clamped\nand overshoot is the point; and endpoints exact for every curve.\n\nWorth noting for ashui: HYBRID_SPRING (0.34, 1.30, 0.64, 1.0) and\nEXPRESSIVE_SPRING (0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1.0) have y2 above 1, so a correct\nevaluate returns values above 1 partway through. Anything clamping to 0..1\ndownstream will flatten the overshoot those curves exist for.\n\n1413 workspace tests pass.","files":null}]}